  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid conditions return this weekend \nwith at least periodic chances for some showers and scattered \nthunderstorms Saturday afternoon through Sunday night.\n\nSurface high pressure will cross New England this morning, then move \noff the east coast later today. A southerly flow will deepen across \nthe region, bringing a warmer and more humid airmass than previous \ndays. \n\nA shortwave trough over the Tennessee Valley will move northeast \nacross the Appalachians and Mid-Atlantic today. A surge of deep \nmoisture, with PWATS averagering 1.6inches (80-90th percentile \nfor this time of year) will enter portions of western and north-\ncentral NY through this evening. This afternoon, weak surface \nbased instability is expected to develop across far western NY. \nShowers and isolated thunderstorms are expected from noon \nthrough the afternoon. Weak low and mid lapse rates and weak \n0-6km shear will support non- severe thunderstorms today. Due to\nthe high moisture content and potential for training storms, \nthere is a low risk of isolated flooding across the western \nSouthern Tier late this afternoon through this evening. HREF \n3-hr PMM suggests the potential for an isolated location \nreceiving an inch of rain in 3 hours across the Southern Tier. \nAs the trough moves eastward this evening, showers and isolated \nthunderstorms are expected to expand into the Finger Lakes \nregion to the southern Tug Hill region, with the threat of \nflooding east of the region. The warm and humid airmass will \npersist ahead of a cold front and isolated to scattered showers \ncan't be ruled out overnight. \n\nA deepening upper level trough will move across the Great Lakes \nregion Sunday. A cold front is expected to move across the forecast \narea. Showers and a few thunderstorms are possible with lingering \ninstability and high dewpoints near the Lakes Sunday morning. Then, \nsurface based instability will develop ahead of the front. Stronger \nwinds aloft will support the potential for an isolated severe\nthunderstorm with the highest probability from the western \nSouthern Tier to the Finger Lakes region/central NY. Forecast\nsoundings suggest damaging winds and large hail would be the  \nmain hazards. Confidence remains low as guidance continues to\nstruggle with the overall coverage of showers and thunderstorms.\nThere remains a chance of showers and a few thunderstorms \nSunday night as the main trough axis swings through the region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool and dry weather expected on Monday, then \nturning warmer Tuesday, with the next chance for some unsettled \nweather arriving by midweek.\n\nAn upper level trough will dig across the Northeast Monday, with \nsurface high pressure building across the area. A cool, northwest \nflow will move into the forecast area with mean 850mb temperatures \nfalling to around +4C Monday. Because the airmass is very dry, \nlittle lake response is expected. An upper level ridge will build \nbriefly across the Great Lakes region Monday night before flattening \nout through mid-week. Warmer and more humid air will move back into \nthe region through mid-week. A frontal boundary may stall near the \nforecast area and bring the potential for periods of showers and \nthunderstorms through mid-week.",
